          #TableOfPPrincipalComponentsInHomotopyGroups.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              The following tables show the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-primary+components"} p-primary components
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} stable homotopy groups of spheres
              for low values, the image of J appears as the bottom row.
            %p
              Here the horizontal index is the degree
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              of the stable homotopy group
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              connected dots vertically above index
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              means that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct summand
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primary+group"} primary group
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+of+a+group"} order
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^k
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InterpretTable"}
              below for illustration. (These tables are taken from (
              %a{:href => "homotopy+groups+of+spheres#Hatcher"}> Hatcher
              ), where in turn they were generated based on (
              %a{:href => "#Ravenel"}> Ravenel 86
              )).
            %p
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 2
                \-primary component
              (e.g.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Ravenel"} Ravenel 86, theorem 3.2.11, figure 4.4.46
            %p
              %img{:alt => "stable homotopy groups of spheres at 2", :src => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/stemfigs/p%3D2pic.gif"}/
            %p
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 3
                \-primary component
            %p
              %img{:alt => "stable homotopy groups of spheres at 3", :src => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/stemfigs/p%3D3pic.gif"}/
            %p
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 5
                \-primary component
            %p
              %img{:alt => "stable homotopy groups of spheres at 5", :src => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/stemfigs/p%3D5pic.gif"}/
          %p We illustrate how to read these tables:
          #InterpretTable.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+abelian+group"} finite abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 24
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_3(\mathbb{S}) \simeq \mathbb{Z}_{24}
              decomposes into
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primary+groups"} primary groups
              as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 8
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq \mathbb{Z}_8 \oplus \mathbb{Z}_3
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 8
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 8 = 2^3
              corresponds to the three dots above
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
              in the first table, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 3
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3 = 3^1
              to the single dot over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
              in the second.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+abelian+group"} finite abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 7
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 240
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_7(\mathbb{S}) \simeq \mathbb{Z}_{240}
              decomposes into
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primary+groups"} primary groups
              as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 16
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq \mathbb{Z}_{16} \oplus \mathbb{Z}_3 \oplus \mathbb{Z}_5
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 16
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 16 = 2^4
              corresponds to the four dots above
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 7
              in the first table, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 3
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3 = 3^1
              to the single dot over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 7
              in the second and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 5
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 5
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5 = 5^1
              to the single dot over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 7
              in the third table.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+abelian+group"} finite abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 11
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 504
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_11(\mathbb{S}) \simeq \mathbb{Z}_{504}
              has
              = succeed "-decomposition" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primary+group"} primary group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mn 3
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 3
                          %mn 2
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \simeq \mathbb{Z}_{2^3} \oplus \mathbb{Z}_{3^2} \oplus \mathbb{Z}_7
              and so this corresponds to the three connected dots over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 11
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 11
              in the first table and the two connected dots over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 11
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 11
              in the second (and there will be one dot over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 11
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 11
              in the fourth table for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 7
              not shown here).
            %p
              The groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
